          'Technology makes tuning systems tractable in a way that was impossible before. The cent — a hundredth of an equal-tempered semitone — gives a common unit for comparing systems precisely: a pure major third is 386 cents against equal temperament’s 400, and a pure fifth is 702 against 700. Those fourteen and two cents are the entire compromise on which Western keyboard music rests. Software tuners and spectrum analysers let you see these differences, and digital instruments let you hear them by retuning at will.',
          'Historical temperaments are not curiosities but the actual sound world of the repertoire you play. Quarter-comma meantone gives pure major thirds in the common keys at the cost of an unusable wolf fifth; well temperaments such as Werckmeister distribute the compromise so every key is playable but each retains a distinct character; equal temperament makes all keys identical and all thirds slightly wide. Playing the same Bach prelude in three temperaments is the fastest way to understand what key character meant to composers before the nineteenth century.',
          'Measurement discipline matters when you use these tools. A tuner reads the fundamental of a complex tone and can be confused by strong overtones or by vibrato; a spectrum analyser shows you the partials and is more informative for timbral questions. Neither replaces the ear in an ensemble, where the target is a beatless interval rather than a number on a display. Use the technology to calibrate your ear in the practice room, and then trust the ear where it counts.',
