          'Technology has removed barriers to music-making that instruments alone could not. Accessible digital musical instruments — from switch-controlled and gaze-controlled systems to adapted acoustic instruments — allow disabled musicians to perform where conventional technique is not possible. Organisations such as Drake Music and the One-Handed Musical Instrument Trust develop and document this work, and the Sounds of Intent framework provides a way of assessing musical development for people with profound and multiple learning difficulties. This is a substantial field with its own research literature, not an afterthought.',
          'Accessibility also covers the ordinary things every musician controls: whether your scores are available in large print or accessible digital formats, whether your recordings are captioned or described, whether your venue can be reached and used by a wheelchair user, whether your rehearsal schedule accommodates people with chronic conditions. Assessing an activity for barriers is a learnable procedure — walk through the whole participant experience and ask at each step who would be excluded and what would remove the exclusion.',
